<BaseLayout title="Conveyor Reject Systems: Reject Belt, Diverter, Pusher" description="Reject belt, reject diverter, pneumatic pusher, or air blast? How each removes flagged packs after a metal detector or checkweigher, with weight limits." canonical="https://packagingconveyor.com/conveyor-functions/reject-systems/" schema={[breadcrumbSchema, articleSchema, faqSchema]}>
<h1>Conveyor Reject Systems</h1>
<p class="usa-intro">A conveyor reject system automatically removes contaminated or out-of-spec product from the line after a metal detector, checkweigher, vision system, or X-ray scanner flags it. The controller tracks the flagged pack to the reject point, a mechanism (air blast, pusher, diverter arm, or retracting belt) moves it into a locked reject bin, and a sensor confirms it left the line. Good product continues downstream without stopping.</p>
<h2 id="how-heading">How a Reject System Works</h2>
<p>A rejection system removes contaminated product from the line in five steps. If any step fails, a properly configured system alarms or stops the line rather than letting the pack continue.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Detect.</strong> The inspection device (metal detector, X-ray, checkweigher, or vision camera) flags the pack and sends a reject signal.</li>
<li><strong>Track.</strong> The controller follows that pack from the inspection point to the reject point, using a belt encoder or a fixed time delay. Encoder tracking holds timing when belt speed changes.</li>
<li><strong>Reject.</strong> The mechanism fires when the pack reaches it and removes only that pack. The mechanism choice is covered in the <a href="#compare-heading">comparison table</a> below.</li>
<li><strong>Confirm.</strong> A photoeye or sensor at the bin or chute verifies the pack actually left the conveyor. No confirmation within the expected window means a failed reject.</li>
<li><strong>Contain and verify.</strong> The bin is locked so rejected product cannot be returned to the line. Bin-full and bin-door sensors stop the line before a full or open bin defeats the system. Operators verify the whole chain with test packs at the start of each shift or at the interval the site's food safety plan sets.</li>
</ol>
