<h2 id="why-heading">Why Reject Systems Matter</h2>
<p>Reject systems are the last line of defense between a defective product and the consumer. A metal detector that finds contamination means nothing if the reject mechanism fails to remove that product from the line. The FDA's <a href="https://www.fda.gov/food/food-safety-modernization-act-fsma/fsma-final-rule-preventive-controls-human-food" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Preventive Controls for Human Food rule</a> requires facilities to verify that their preventive controls work, and GFSI-benchmarked schemes (SQF, BRCGS, FSSC 22000) audit whether detection and reject systems are tested and recorded. <a href="https://www.brcgs.com/media/2164733/brcgs-foodissue8faqsjuly2020.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">BRCGS guidance</a> describes testing with test packs to check each function: detection, the reject mechanism or line stop, and authorised restart.</p>
<p>Reject system failures cause two types of costly events. <strong>False passes</strong>, contaminated product reaches the consumer, triggering recalls, liability, and brand damage. <strong>False rejects</strong>, good product diverts into the reject bin, wasting material and reducing yield. A well-designed reject system minimizes both. The detection device determines false pass and false reject rates. The reject mechanism determines whether a true detection results in reliable product removal.</p>
<p>Most <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food packaging lines</a> place reject systems at two to four points: after the metal detector (mandatory for most retailers), after the checkweigher (weight compliance), after the vision system (label and package integrity), and optionally after the X-ray system (foreign object detection). Each reject point requires its own mechanism, bin, and confirmation sensor.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/reject-loma-iq4-cw3-combo.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="A stainless steel inspection line with a metal detector head over the belt at left, a checkweigher touchscreen cabinet in the middle, and two enclosed reject bin cabinets side by side at right." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Loma IQ4 metal detector and CW3 checkweigher combination system, with two reject bin cabinets after the checkweigher. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.loma.com/en-us/products/combination-systems/iq4-cw3-combo" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Loma Systems</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h2 id="types-heading">Reject Mechanism Types</h2>
<p>A conveyor reject mechanism is the device that physically removes a flagged pack: an air nozzle, a pusher, a diverter arm, or an opening in the conveyor bed. Most are pneumatic (air blast, pneumatic pushers, pneumatic diverter arms), with servo drives used where stroke length or cycle rate must be programmable. For pushers and diverters used outside inspection, such as lane sorting, see <a href="/conveyor-functions/pusher-diverter/">pusher and diverter mechanisms</a>.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Reject belt:</strong> a conveyor section that retracts, drops, or pivots down to open a gap, so the flagged pack falls into a bin below. No side force touches the pack.</li>
<li><strong>Reject diverter:</strong> a pivoting arm, gate, or plough that swings across the belt and steers the flagged pack off the side into a bin or onto a reject lane, then swings back.</li>
<li><strong>Pneumatic pusher:</strong> an air cylinder drives a blade across the belt and pushes the pack off the side.</li>
<li><strong>Air blast:</strong> a solenoid valve fires an air nozzle that blows a light pack off the belt.</li>
</ul>
<p>Weight limits below are one manufacturer's published figures (<a href="https://www.loma.com/en-us/about-us/news-press-releases/what-reject-system-is-right-for-my-application" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Loma Systems reject guide</a>) and show the typical order of capability. Confirm limits for your pack with your supplier.</p>
<h3>Air Blast Reject</h3>
<p>Air blast rejects use a burst of compressed air to blow product off the conveyor into a reject bin. A solenoid valve fires one or more air nozzles mounted beside or above the conveyor. It is the fastest reject type and lets the line keep running. Loma lists a maximum pack weight of 0.8 kg for its air blast reject and does not recommend it for open or breakable packaging.</p>
<p>Air blast works best for small, lightweight items: individual snack bags, pouches, candy bars, blister packs, and loose confectionery. The air pulse must generate enough force to push the product clear of the belt before the next product arrives. Product spacing (gap between products) must be wide enough that the air blast does not deflect adjacent good product. If the gap is shorter than the distance the belt travels during the air pulse, the neighboring pack is exposed to the blast and may be rejected with the bad one.</p>
<p>Limitations: air blast cannot reject heavy products (cases, bottles, jars). Compressed air consumption adds operating cost, a high-reject-rate line uses significant air volume. In dusty environments, the air blast can scatter particulates. Air blast rejects need a clean, dry, filtered compressed air supply to prevent weak blasts and nozzle clogging. Use the filtration and pressure the supplier specifies.</p>
<h3>Conveyor Reject Pusher (Pusher Arm)</h3>
<p>A conveyor reject pusher uses a pneumatic or servo-driven cylinder to push the flagged pack off the side of the conveyor, and it is the most common reject for cases and rigid containers. The arm extends across the belt, contacts the product, and pushes it into a reject chute or bin. It is the workhorse reject for cases, cartons, bottles, jars, and cans. Loma rates its standard pusher to 10 kg and offers a heavy-duty pusher for packs up to 50 kg at a maximum of 15 packs per minute.</p>
<p>Pusher arm design must match the product's height, width, and center of gravity. The contact pad (the surface that touches the product) should be wide enough to push the product without tipping it. Products with a high center of gravity (tall bottles, upright cartons) may need a two-level pusher, one pad contacts the base, another contacts the upper portion.</p>
<p>The pusher arm must fully retract before the next good product arrives. At high speeds with tight product spacing, the retract time limits throughput. Servo-driven pushers retract faster than pneumatic and offer programmable stroke length for different product widths. Servo pushers cost more than pneumatic ones because of the servo motor, drive, and motion programming.</p>
<h3>Reject Diverter (Diverter Arm / Sweep Gate)</h3>
<p>A reject diverter is a pivoting arm or gate that swings across the conveyor path and steers the flagged pack off the side. The gate pivots from one side of the conveyor and sweeps product to the opposite side into a reject chute, then reopens for good product. It is slower than an air blast or pusher because the arm must swing out and back. Capacity varies widely by design: Loma's plough/diverter reject for inspection conveyors is rated to 1 kg, while heavy case and tray diverters are built for much heavier loads.</p>
<p><strong>In-line diverter rejection</strong> keeps the rejected pack on a conveyor instead of dropping it in a bin: the arm or a guide rail steers it into a parallel reject lane or onto a reject table. This suits bottles and cans that would tip or break in a bin, and cases that operators must inspect or rework. Where the defect is a contamination risk, the reject lane still needs a locked or guarded end point.</p>
<p>Diverter gates excel at rejecting products that arrive in groups (multi-packs, shrink-wrapped bundles) because the sweeping motion clears the full belt width. The gate design can include a curved guide surface that redirects product smoothly rather than pushing against it, reducing impact damage to the product and the mechanism.</p>
<p>For <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/pallet/">pallet conveyor</a> applications, heavy-duty diverter gates reject entire cases or pallets. These gates use servo motors or hydraulic cylinders rather than pneumatics to handle the high forces required.</p>
<h3>Reject Belt: Retracting Nose, Drop-Out &amp; Drop-Flap</h3>
<p>A reject belt (also called a retracting, drop-out, or drop-flap reject) opens a gap in the conveyor bed so the flagged pack falls through into a bin below, with no side force on the product. Drop-out belt rejects remove a section of the conveyor belt when a defect triggers rejection. The belt section either drops away (hinged), retracts laterally, or accelerates forward to open a gap. Product falls through the opening into a reject bin below the conveyor. It is the slowest pack-level method, but with a key advantage: no lateral force touches the product. Loma rates both its carriage retract band and its power declining belt to 5 kg, with a 100 mm maximum pack height on the declining belt.</p>
<figure class="video"><a class="video__link" href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Q6d2bO-k4CI" data-youtube="Q6d2bO-k4CI"><img src="/img/photos/yt-q6d2bok4ci.webp" width="1000" height="563" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><span class="video__play">Play video: Products rejecting on the IQ4 Carriage Retract Band</span></a><figcaption>Loma shows a range of products being rejected by the carriage retract band on its IQ4 metal detector conveyor. <span class="photo-credit">Video: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/@lomasystems" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">LOMA SYSTEMS</a> on YouTube</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Drop-out belts suit products that cannot tolerate side pushing, open trays, unwrapped food, loose product on a belt. Gravity does the work, so pack shape and stability matter less than with side-push methods, but the belt mechanism still sets a weight limit. The drop-out section must be long enough to clear the entire product before closing. A drop-flap is the bulk-flow version: a hinged flap at a transfer point or chute swings open to dump a slug of loose product (nuts, cereal, frozen pieces) around the detected contaminant.</p>
<p>The primary limitation is speed. The belt must open, the product must clear the gap, and the belt must close before the next product arrives. At high speeds with tight product spacing, drop-out belts cannot cycle fast enough. They need the longest gap between packs of any reject type. Get the minimum gap from the supplier for your pack length and belt speed.</p>
<h2 id="compare-heading">Reject Mechanism Comparison</h2>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Mechanism</th>
<th>Product Types</th>
<th>Line Speed Fit</th>
<th>Product Contact</th>
<th>Cost Drivers</th>
<th>Common Failure Modes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Air Blast</td>
<td>Light packs: snack bags, pouches, bars, blister packs</td>
<td>Highest</td>
<td>None (air only)</td>
<td>Valve and nozzles, compressed air use at high reject rates</td>
<td>Low or wet air supply weakens the blast. Tight gaps blow good neighbors off. Pack weight or shape changes leave product on the belt.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Pusher Arm</td>
<td>Cartons, cases, bottles, jars, cans</td>
<td>Medium to high (servo faster than pneumatic)</td>
<td>Side contact</td>
<td>Cylinder or servo drive, guarding, chute and bin</td>
<td>Timing drift hits the wrong pack edge or tips tall product. Slow retract strikes the next pack. Packs jam in the reject chute.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Diverter Arm / Sweep Gate</td>
<td>Cases, trays, bundles, multi-packs, in-line reject lanes</td>
<td>Low to medium</td>
<td>Sweep contact</td>
<td>Arm size and drive force, reject lane or table if product stays upright</td>
<td>Arm returns late and takes the next good pack. Product bridges or wedges at the gate. Pivot wear changes sweep timing.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Retracting / Drop-Out Belt</td>
<td>Open trays, unwrapped or fragile food, mixed weights</td>
<td>Low to medium (needs long gaps)</td>
<td>None (gravity)</td>
<td>Retracting belt mechanism, drive, wear parts, space under the belt</td>
<td>Short packs carry over the gap. Belt closes on a pack still falling. Nose roller and belt wear from constant cycling.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Drop-Flap</td>
<td>Loose or bulk product in a continuous stream</td>
<td>Continuous flow (rejects a slug, not one pack)</td>
<td>None (gravity)</td>
<td>Chute or transfer redesign, lost good product per reject</td>
<td>Flap opens too briefly and the contaminant passes. Product lodges in the hinge. Rejects more good product than pack-level methods.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Stop-on-Detect (Belt Stop)</td>
<td>Low-rate, large, or irregular product</td>
<td>Low</td>
<td>Manual removal</td>
<td>Lowest hardware cost, highest labor and downtime per reject</td>
<td>Depends on the operator removing the right item. Often not acceptable where retailer or audit codes call for automatic rejection.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>For cost planning across the line, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</p>
<h2 id="integration-heading">Inspection Device Integration</h2>
<h3>Metal Detector Integration</h3>
<p>Metal detectors scan product for ferrous, non-ferrous, and stainless steel contamination. The detector sends a reject signal to the reject mechanism controller after detecting metal. The time delay between detection and reject activation accounts for the distance the product must travel from the detector aperture to the reject point. This delay, called the reject delay or product track time, must be calculated precisely. Too short, and the reject fires before the contaminated product arrives. Too long, and the product passes the reject point.</p>
<p>Most metal detector suppliers sell combination units that mount the detector, conveyor belt, and reject mechanism on one frame. This fixes the detector-to-reject distance at the factory and removes most integration timing issues.</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/reject-loma-iq4-conveyor.webp" width="1000" height="1000" alt="A stainless steel conveyor with a blue modular belt passing through a metal detector head with a touchscreen, followed by an enclosed reject station with a bin cabinet, all on one frame." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>Loma IQ4 conveyorized metal detector system: detector head, belt conveyor, and enclosed reject station with bin cabinet on one frame. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://www.loma.com/en-us/products/metal-detection/iq4-conveyor-system" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Loma Systems</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Checkweigher Integration</h3>
<p>Checkweighers weigh every product in motion and reject units outside the target weight range (underweight or overweight). The checkweigher's built-in reject system, typically a pusher arm or air blast, fires immediately after the weigh cell determines the product is out of spec. Checkweighers require a controlled infeed spacing to ensure only one product occupies the weigh cell at a time. An <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing conveyor</a> or spacing belt upstream provides this consistent product gap.</p>
<p>Checkweighers are commonly sold with integrated reject systems and reject confirmation sensors. The confirmation sensor verifies that the rejected product left the belt and entered the reject bin. If confirmation fails, the checkweigher alarms and optionally stops the line, a critical safety feature for products sold by weight (meat, cheese, produce).</p>
<figure class="photo"><img src="/img/photos/reject-ap-dataweigh-checkweigher.webp" width="1000" height="641" alt="A three-belt in-motion checkweigher with a touchscreen display on a post, feeding a separate station that has a stainless tray and an overhead actuator on a gantry frame." loading="lazy" decoding="async" /><figcaption>AP Dataweigh three-bed checkweigher with a reject and accumulation station at the discharge end. <span class="photo-credit">Photo: <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AP_Dataweigh_3_bed_checkweigher_with_touchscreen_reject_and_accumulation_station.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Svanduzen, CC BY-SA 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons</a></span></figcaption></figure>
<h3>Vision System Integration</h3>
<p><a href="/conveyor-functions/inspection-detection/">Vision inspection systems</a> check labels (presence, position, readability, content), package integrity (seal quality, fill level, cap presence), and cosmetic defects (color, surface damage). The vision camera captures an image, the software processes it, and the system sends a pass/fail signal to the reject controller. Vision rejects require precise product tracking because the camera and reject point are often well apart on the conveyor.</p>
<p>Product tracking uses either an encoder (measuring belt travel distance from camera to reject) or a photoeye (detecting product arrival at the reject point). Encoder-based tracking is more reliable at variable speeds. The reject controller calculates when the defective product will reach the reject mechanism and fires at the correct moment. For systems integrating vision with other inspection devices, see the <a href="/resources/automated-conveyor-systems/">automated conveyor systems</a> guide.</p>
<h3>X-Ray System Integration</h3>
<p>X-ray inspection detects foreign objects that metal detectors miss, glass, stone, bone, dense plastics, and rubber. X-ray systems also perform mass estimation, fill level checking, and component counting. Reject mechanisms for X-ray systems follow the same principles as metal detector rejects, and X-ray units are also sold with integrated conveyor and reject.</p>
<h2 id="confirm-heading">Reject Confirmation &amp; Compliance</h2>
<p>Reject confirmation is the process of verifying that a rejected product actually left the main conveyor. A sensor (photoeye or proximity switch) monitors the reject bin or chute. When the reject mechanism fires, the confirmation sensor expects to detect a product entering the reject bin within a defined time window. Three outcomes are possible:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Confirmed reject:</strong> The sensor detects the product in the bin. The system logs the event and continues running. Normal operation.</li>
<li><strong>Failed reject (no confirmation):</strong> The sensor does not detect a product in the bin. The contaminated product may still be on the main conveyor. The system triggers an alarm and, depending on configuration, stops the line. This is the critical safety event that reject confirmation exists to catch.</li>
<li><strong>False confirmation:</strong> The sensor detects a product in the bin, but it was not the target product (e.g., a different product fell into the bin accidentally). Rare, but possible if the reject bin sensor lacks specificity.</li>
</ul>
<p>BRCGS guidance describes failsafe systems that monitor the detector's own functions and raise an alarm when one stops working. Its example is a reject powered by compressed air: if the air supply fails, the alarm sounds immediately instead of the fault waiting for the next scheduled check. Sites are expected to check that these failsafes work. Retailer codes of practice often set more specific requirements, such as automatic line stop on a failed confirmation. Check each customer's code before you specify.</p>
<p>The reject bin must be locked to prevent operators from retrieving rejected product and returning it to the line. Keyed locks, electronic latches tied to the PLC, and tamper-evident seals all serve this purpose. Locked bins with confirmation sensors are a common option on <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">food-grade inspection systems</a>.</p>
<h2 id="design-heading">Conveyor Design for Reject Zones</h2>
<p>The conveyor section carrying product through the reject zone requires specific design features that differ from standard transport conveyors:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Consistent speed:</strong> Product tracking from detector to reject relies on known belt speed. VFD-controlled conveyors with encoder feedback hold speed closely, and encoder tracking keeps reject timing correct when speed does change. Speed variation causes missed rejects or good-product rejects.</li>
<li><strong>Adequate product spacing:</strong> Each product must be separated by enough gap to allow the reject mechanism to fire and retract between products. Minimum gap depends on the mechanism: air blast needs the least, pushers need more to retract, and reject belts need the most. Get the figure from the supplier for your pack length and belt speed. A spacing conveyor or <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing section</a> upstream creates the required gap.</li>
<li><strong>Side-clearance for pusher rejects:</strong> The reject side of the conveyor needs clear space for the reject chute and bin. The opposite side needs an open path for the pusher arm stroke. Conveyor frame height must allow the reject bin to fit underneath or beside the belt.</li>
<li><strong>Washdown construction:</strong> Reject zones in <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary food environments</a> need stainless steel frames, IP69K motors, and belt designs that allow tool-free removal for cleaning. Reject bin interiors must be accessible for sanitation.</li>
</ul>
<p>The conveyor section immediately downstream of the reject must not accumulate product during a reject event. If the reject mechanism removes a product, the resulting gap in the product stream should flow through to the next machine without causing a fault. <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">Accumulation conveyors</a> downstream absorb the intermittent gaps created by rejects.</p>
<h2 id="selection-heading">Selecting a Reject System</h2>
<p>Match the reject mechanism to five parameters:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Product weight and size.</strong> Air blast for light packs. Pushers for most cases, cartons, and rigid containers, with heavy-duty versions for heavy packs. Diverters where the pack should stay upright or move to a reject lane. Reject belts for packs that cannot tolerate side contact, within the belt's weight limit. Use each supplier's rated weight limit.</li>
<li><strong>Conveyor speed and product spacing.</strong> Higher speed demands faster mechanism response. Calculate the product transit time from detector to reject: Transit time = Distance (inches) / Speed (inches/sec). The mechanism must complete its cycle within this window.</li>
<li><strong>Reject rate.</strong> High reject rates mean the mechanism cycles frequently. Pneumatic mechanisms consume more compressed air; electrical mechanisms consume more power. High reject rates also fill the reject bin faster, size the bin for the expected reject volume per shift.</li>
<li><strong>Compliance requirements.</strong> Food and pharmaceutical applications require reject confirmation sensors, locked reject bins, and event logging. Ensure the reject system's data output integrates with your line PLC and quality management system.</li>
<li><strong>Environment.</strong> <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">Washdown environments</a> require stainless steel construction and IP69K ratings. Dry environments accept standard industrial construction.</li>
</ol>
<p>For cost planning across your entire conveyor system, including reject stations, see the <a href="/resources/conveyor-cost-guide/">conveyor cost guide</a>.</p>
<SpecChecklist id="spec-checklist" title="Reject system specification checklist" items={[
  ["Inspection device", "Type (metal detector, X-ray, checkweigher, vision), make and model, and how it sends the reject signal."],
  ["Pack data", "Dimensions, weight range, packaging type, and whether packs are open, fragile, tall, or unstable."],
  ["Line speed and spacing", "Belt speed range and the minimum gap between packs at the reject point."],
  ["Reject mechanism", "Preferred type (air blast, pusher, diverter, reject belt, drop flap, or stop on detect) and the supplier's rated weight limit for your pack."],
  ["Tracking", "Encoder or timer tracking, and the distance from the inspection point to the reject point."],
  ["Confirmation and fail-safes", "Reject confirm sensor, bin-full and bin-door sensors, low air pressure alarm, and whether each fault alarms or stops the line."],
  ["Reject bin", "Locked or secured bin, capacity per shift, and who controls access."],
  ["Utilities", "Compressed air quality and pressure available at the machine, and electrical supply."],
