<h2 id="faq-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="usa-accordion usa-accordion--bordered usa-accordion--multiselectable" data-allow-multiple="">
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="true" aria-controls="faq-1">What is a conveyor reject system?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-1" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A conveyor reject system removes non-conforming product from the line after a metal detector, checkweigher, vision system, or X-ray scanner detects a defect. The mechanism fires when the tracked pack reaches it and moves the defective product into a locked reject bin while good product continues downstream.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-2">How do rejection systems remove contaminated products from the line?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-2" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>A rejection system removes contaminated product in five steps. An inspection device (metal detector, X-ray, checkweigher, or vision system) flags the pack. The controller tracks that pack by belt encoder or timer to the reject point. A reject mechanism (air blast, pusher, diverter arm, or retracting belt) removes it into a reject bin. A confirm sensor verifies the pack left the line. The bin is locked so rejected product cannot be returned, and any failed step stops the line or raises an alarm.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-3">What types of reject mechanisms exist?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-3" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>The main reject mechanisms are: (1) <strong>Air blast</strong>, a compressed air nozzle blows light packs off the belt. It is the fastest type, and Loma lists a maximum pack weight of 0.8 kg for its air blast. (2) <strong>Pusher</strong>, a pneumatic or servo blade pushes the pack off the side into a bin. Loma rates its standard pusher to 10 kg. (3) <strong>Reject diverter</strong>, a pivoting arm or gate swings across the belt and steers the pack off the side or into a reject lane. (4) <strong>Reject belt</strong>, a retracting or dropping belt section opens a gap so the pack falls into a bin below with no side force. Loma rates its retracting band to 5 kg. Stop on detect, which stops the belt for manual removal, is used for very large packs. The right choice depends on pack weight, line speed, and whether the pack tolerates side contact.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-4">How fast must a reject system respond?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-4" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>The controller must fire the reject when the flagged pack reaches the reject point, and the mechanism must finish its stroke and reset before the next good pack arrives. For example, at 200 FPM (40 inches per second), a pack travels 12 inches in 0.3 seconds. Air blast is the fastest mechanism, pushers and diverters are slower, and reject belts are slowest because the belt must open and close. Get the cycle time for each mechanism from the supplier and check it against your belt speed and pack gap.</p>

</div>
<h3 class="usa-accordion__heading"><button type="button" class="usa-accordion__button" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="faq-5">Do I need a reject confirmation sensor?</button></h3>
<div id="faq-5" class="usa-accordion__content usa-prose">
<p>For food and pharmaceutical lines and for contamination defects such as metal, a confirm sensor is standard good practice. It verifies that the flagged pack left the conveyor and entered the reject bin. If it does not confirm within the expected window, the system alarms and stops the line. BRCGS guidance describes failsafe systems that alarm when a reject function fails, such as loss of compressed air, and expects sites to check them. Many retailer codes of practice add specific fail-safe requirements, so check your customers' codes.</p>

</div>
</div>
